 lives others who  say they just cannot work under such  conditions as the international  federation of journalists puts it  afghanistan risks becoming a news desert  the listening posts meenakshi ravi now  with the story of afghan journalists and  where they find themselves six months  after the taliban takeover  [Music]  a number of us female journalists had  groups on whatsapp and facebook  messenger  i got a text in a group saying that the  taliban had entered kabul  no one ever believed that all of a  sudden kabul the capital city of  afghanistan would also be under the  control of the taliban  after the collapse of the government i  stayed in kabul for another month i  thought the situation for the media  would be all right i thought the taliban  might have really changed  unfortunately though one and a half  months after the collapse of the  government i just lost hope  it was the 8th of september 2021 less  than a month after the taliban had swept  into kabul and took power  a small group of women gathered to  protest  two journalists from the investigative  newspaper ethelat rose were covering the  demonstration when they were picked up  by taliban security  four hours later the men were released  the extent of their beating was clear  for zaki daryabi the founder and editor  of the paper it was evident afghanistan  was no longer a place for journalists  something i really was not expecting was  the kind of violence torture and beating  that happened to my colleagues i never  thought they would come out of prison in  this state  it is very hard for me to explain the  impact of losing the atellatrous office  my colleagues there and also losing the  freedom to do our work  i really can't go into  many afghan journalists who left  afghanistan after the fall of kabul  had very legitimate  reasons to  fear for their lives  given that in the past just one year  until the fall of kabul the level of  targeted killings  focused on civil society members  journalists 