t but it was not  an easy process  whether it was during hamid karzai's  presidency  we always had a lot of people being  targeted in the afghan media family  but now after 15th of august 2021  whatever freedom of speech was achieved  in the last two decades is really in  danger  today the situation is such that there  is no motivation to work in the media  afghanistan  it's always been very difficult to be  journalists in afghanistan and at the  moment difficulties are even more  especially for a woman journalist it's  difficult to go out to ask questions to  be in the public space but we do what we  can but again the the issue here is the  lack of finance and the lack of revenue  for us we are desperate and i tried to  knock to all doors but the response from  the international donors is so slow and  it's so bureaucratic we are in hurry and  we need desperately  to find a solution  after the government was taken over by  the taliban  all the structures of the media industry  collapsed  there is no legal support for  journalists  access has been badly limited and the  authority of the media is almost  completely lost  journalists had earned some trust in  society we were able to ask for  accountability from the government  the fall of kabul has taken all of this  from the media  total news decided  rightfully so to stay in afghanistan and  continue their work unfortunately some  of my colleagues had to leave  afghanistan after the fall but the  institution  survived  and hired new journalists but it's not  the same i can't say  it's the same afghanistan you know the  sense of fear is there but in the same  time  demonstration of bravery is also there a  lot has changed  but even if we get a much lesser number  of genuine stories coming out of  afghanistan it's better than nothing  nothing or at least very little of value  getting reported isn't an exaggerated  fear  consider what journalists in afghanistan  are up against  hey hey hey hey  calm down media workers a